1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,840
 Welcome back. In this lesson, we're going to answer a very important question.

2
00:00:03,840 --> 00:00:09,360
 What is "transplation"? Are you going to hear this term a lot when working with TypeScript?

3
00:00:09,360 --> 00:00:14,720
 Modern JavaScript and front-end tools. By the end of this lesson, you're going to clearly

4
00:00:14,720 --> 00:00:19,920
 understand what "transplation" is, why it exists, and how TypeScript uses it.

5
00:00:19,920 --> 00:00:27,600
 JavaScript evolves constantly. New features are added every year, things like area functions, classes,

6
00:00:27,600 --> 00:00:32,800
 asynchronous, but the problem is not all environments support the latest JavaScript features,

7
00:00:32,800 --> 00:00:39,120
 including older browsers or older versions of Node.js, perhaps embedded or legacy systems.

8
00:00:39,120 --> 00:00:44,960
 If we write a modern JavaScript or TypeScript, older environments might not understand it,

9
00:00:44,960 --> 00:00:50,560
 and that's where "transplation" comes in. The "transplation" means converting the code from

10
00:00:50,560 --> 00:00:56,880
 one version of our language into another version of the same language. In simple terms,

11
00:00:56,880 --> 00:01:00,400
 modern code goes in, and a compatible code comes out.

12
00:01:00,400 --> 00:01:06,800
 So let's clear up a common confusion. Transplation is different from compilation.

13
00:01:06,800 --> 00:01:13,840
 Compilation is TypeScript, plus JavaScript, whereas "transplation" is ES6 JavaScript,

14
00:01:13,840 --> 00:01:20,560
 plus ES5 JavaScript. Compilation usually means converting one language into a different

15
00:01:20,560 --> 00:01:24,960
 language, whereas "transplation" converts the same language into a different version.

16
00:01:25,920 --> 00:01:29,760
 TypeScript actually does both. It compiles TypeScript into JavaScript,

17
00:01:29,760 --> 00:01:34,880
 and it transmiles modern JavaScript features into older ones, if needed.

18
00:01:34,880 --> 00:01:39,280
 Let's see "transplation" in action using plain old JavaScript.

19
00:01:39,280 --> 00:01:47,600
 So here we have some straightforward code, "const" and "equals" in brackets, "a" as a type of number,

20
00:01:47,600 --> 00:01:54,240
 "b" as a type of number, "a" plus "b". So here's a bit of TypeScript,

21
00:01:54,240 --> 00:02:00,000
 using an area function, a modern JavaScript feature. After "transplation" older JavaScript,

22
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:04,800
 it might look like this. The logic is the same, but the syntax has been transformed

23
00:02:04,800 --> 00:02:10,640
 so that older environments can understand it. Now let's have a look at how TypeScript uses

24
00:02:10,640 --> 00:02:18,720
 "transplation". Here's a typical TypeScript code. This is modern TypeScript using classes

25
00:02:18,720 --> 00:02:24,560
 and template strings. When we run the TypeScript compiler, the code is transpiled into JavaScript.

26
00:02:24,560 --> 00:02:31,600
 This JavaScript works in much older environments, even though the original code uses modern syntax.

27
00:02:31,600 --> 00:02:38,640
 So why is "transplation" important? "transplation" gives us three major benefits.

28
00:02:38,640 --> 00:02:44,160
 It allows us to write "modern" clean code, "support" older environments,

29
00:02:44,880 --> 00:02:51,040
 and "catch" errors early. We get the best of both worlds, modern developer experience

30
00:02:51,040 --> 00:02:58,400
 and broad compatibility. Another important point, "transplation" happens before your code runs.

31
00:02:58,400 --> 00:03:04,160
 The browser never sees TypeScript, it only sees the final JavaScript output.

32
00:03:04,160 --> 00:03:11,200
 Some popular tools to perform "transplation" include the TypeScript compiler itself,

33
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:19,680
 "babble", "SWC", and "ES build". Even if you don't configure these tools directly,

34
00:03:19,680 --> 00:03:23,360
 frameworks like React, Angular and View use them behind the scenes.

35
00:03:23,360 --> 00:03:31,280
 When you don't need "transplation". You might not need "transplation" if you only

36
00:03:31,280 --> 00:03:37,360
 target "modern browsers". You control the runtime environment or you don't use modern features.

37
00:03:38,560 --> 00:03:42,000
 But in most real-world projects, "transplation" is essential.

38
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:47,200
 To summarize, "transplation" converts "modern" code into "compatible" code.

39
00:03:47,200 --> 00:03:52,640
 It keeps the language the same. TypeScript relies heavily on "transplation",

40
00:03:52,640 --> 00:03:58,560
 and it allows us to write clean "modern" code without worrying about compatibility.

